## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates practical design skills for privacy-preserving NLP systems, edge computing constraints, and multilingual text processing in real-world chat applications.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid PII detection using regex rules and ML classification
- On-device inference with strict latency requirements (<150ms)
- Multilingual support and code-switching handling
- Configurable redaction policies and comprehensive audit logging
- Evaluation methodology combining offline metrics and staged rollout
- Automated rollback strategies for production safety

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for multilingual PII redaction
function redactPII(text, language = 'auto') {
  const tokens = tokenizeMultilingual(text);
  const regexMatches = applyRegexRules(tokens);
  const mlPredictions = classifyContext(tokens, language);
  return mergeAndRedact(text, regexMatches, mlPredictions);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you balance false positives versus coverage in PII redaction?
- How would you monitor and maintain model performance across languages?
- What fallback mechanisms would you implement for edge cases?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to enable rapid domain adaptation with privacy guarantees in a real-time environment, emphasizing latency constraints, privacy preservation, and robust rollback mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- Parameter-efficient adapters (LoRA/Adapters) for domain adaptation
- On-device fine-tuning with privacy guarantees (DP or federated learning)
- Streaming drift detection and safe rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: load base model, attach adapter for domain, fine-tune on small labeled set
function adaptDomain(model, domain, labeledData) {
  const adapter = new LoRAAdapter(model, { rank: 4, alpha: 8 });
  adapter.fineTune(labeledData, { privacy: 'DP-SGD', epochs: 3 });
  return adapter;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Regulated, large-scale NLP systems require auditable provenance, per-tenant governance, and secure artifact management. This question probes how to architect for verifiable model provenance, drift detection, and safe rollouts across tenants and languages.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant enclaves and policy evaluation
- Cryptographic signing and KMS/HSM
- Immutable audit trail with Merkle proofs
- Data lineage and privacy controls
- Drift detection across tenants
- Safe rollout and rollback (canary/blue-green)
- Governance and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: sign artifact with KMS and record hash in ledger
function signArtifact(artifact, kmsClient, ledger) {
  const hash = hashArtifact(artifact);
  const signature = kmsClient.sign(hash);
  ledger.append({ hash, signature, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return signature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale provenance checks as tenants scale to thousands?
- How would you verify rollback integrity without downtime in a live channel?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical NLP task: normalize product names across languages in chat, a common customer-support need.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual dictionary-based normalization
- Code-switching handling via token-level language tagging
- Lightweight fuzzy matching (Levenshtein) for typos
- In-browser latency constraints and basic privacy controls
- Per-language evaluation plan

## Code Example

```javascript
const dict = {
  en: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" },
  es: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" },
  de: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" }
};

function normalize(token, lang){
  const d = dict[lang] || {};
  if (d[token]) return { canonical_id: d[token], conf: 1.0 };
  // simple fallback: lowercasing + basic similarity could be added here
  return { canonical_id: token, conf: 0.5 };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to add new product names without retraining?
- How to detect language and manage code-switching at runtime?
- How to scale dictionaries across dozens of languages while preserving privacy?
